speechtext:
They do not go there; I do not know why, but they have never done so. Last year 446 immigrants were examined in Montreal, which has not the facilities to take care of the immigration flow. It would be a great mistake to have two ports equipped on the St. Lawrence for handling immigrants.
Now, why should we not take steps to cope with the immense trade which, in my opinion at any rate, is surely coming to the port of Quebec? Why should we not provide facilities even two years in advance? As a matter of fact, there are not adequate facilities for this very summer. The harbour board is composed of men of outstanding reputation in the business and commercial world and they have submitted a plan and explained it, supporting it by every possible argument that could be advanced. And that is not all; the shipping federation of Canada has endorsed the scheme. If all the facilities are available, if all the shipping companies at present have everything that they can wish for, then I ask pertinently, why should they enthusiastically and forcibly endorse the scheme? Is that all? It is not. What about the president of the National Railways, Sir Henry Thornton? In a memorandum written in March, 1924 he supported this very development. What about the president of the Canadian Pacific Railway, Mr. Beatty? He, too, in a memorandum endorses this scheme. Does this mean that the Canadian National Railways and the Canadian Pacific Railway touch Quebec only? No, both systems have terminal? in Montreal;

but they also have terminals in the city of Quebec. So it is not a case of favouring Quebec as against Montreal, or of favouring the development of one part of the country as against another part. It is because in their hearts those gentlemen are convinced that those developments are in the national interest that they endorse them. Certainly anything that is in the interests of the railways is. in almost every instance, in the interests of the country generally.
